  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>On the palm side of the wrist there is a canal. The finger tendons run along its floor, a dense ligament covers it from above, and the <strong>median nerve</strong> lies between them. The nerve gives feeling to the thumb, index, middle and half of the ring finger and controls the muscles at the base of the thumb.</p>
    <CarpalFig kind="section" lang="en" />
    <p>If there is too little room in the canal — because of swollen tendons, a thickened ligament or an injury — the nerve gets squeezed. It is like a cable caught in a door: less and less current gets through, and the fingers stop feeling. This is carpal tunnel syndrome, the most common entrapment syndrome.</p>
    <NerveMap limb="arm" focus="carpal" lang="en" />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Numbness and tingling</strong> in the thumb, index, middle and part of the ring finger. The little finger is usually spared — if it is the one going numb, think of the ulnar nerve.</li>
      <li><strong>Waking at night</strong> because the hand has "gone dead". Many shake the hand or hang it off the bed — it helps.</li>
      <li><strong>Pain and burning</strong> in the hand, sometimes radiating to the forearm.</li>
      <li><strong>Clumsiness and weakness</strong>: hard to do up buttons, hold a phone or a mug; things fall from the hands.</li>
      <li>In advanced cases — <strong>wasting of the muscles</strong> at the base of the thumb. This is a sign of long-standing compression, and further delay is not advisable.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It usually starts with night episodes that pass quickly. Over time numbness lasts longer, appears during the day, and then weakness joins in.</p>
    <h3>Who is at risk</h3>
    <p>Women are affected noticeably more often than men — especially with hormonal changes: during pregnancy, menopause, thyroid disease. The risk is also higher in people whose hands do repetitive work for years — office workers, cooks, hairdressers, manicurists, drivers, musicians — and with diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis and after wrist fractures.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>How the diagnosis is confirmed</h2>
    <p>Hand numbness is not only caused by entrapment. Similar sensations come from a cervical disc herniation, vitamin B12 deficiency, diabetic polyneuropathy and vascular problems. So the diagnosis is based on examination and tests together, not on a single symptom.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Examination by a neurologist or neurosurgeon.</strong> I check sensation and hand strength and do provocative tests — for example Phalen's test: with the wrist flexed, numbness increases within a minute.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve conduction study</strong> — the main method. It shows whether conduction along the median nerve is slowed and how badly the nerve is affected.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve ultrasound</strong> — shows swelling of the nerve before the compression and helps rule out other causes such as cysts or tumours.</li>
      <li><strong>Cervical spine MRI</strong> — if the cause is suspected higher up, in the spine.</li>
    </ul>
    <CarpalFig kind="phalen" lang="en" />
    <CarpalFig kind="enmg" lang="en" />
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Non-surgical treatment</h2>
    <p>If symptoms are recent, there is no muscle weakness and the nerve conduction study shows mild changes, I start with conservative treatment:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A night splint</strong> that keeps the wrist in a neutral position and stops it bending during sleep.</li>
      <li><strong>Less strain</strong>: a comfortable hand position at the keyboard and mouse, breaks in repetitive work.</li>
      <li><strong>Medication</strong> — anti-inflammatory and other drugs; the specific drugs and course are prescribed by a doctor.</li>
      <li><strong>Physiotherapy and exercises</strong>. <a href="#uprazhneniya">Five simple hand exercises</a> are below.</li>
      <li><strong>An injection</strong> of an anti-inflammatory drug into the canal area. It reduces swelling, but in some patients the effect is temporary.</li>
    </ul>
    <details class="fold" id="uprazhneniya">
      <summary>Five hand exercises</summary>
      <div class="fold-body">
        <ol>
          <li><strong>“Prayer”.</strong> Put your palms together in front of your chest. Slowly lower your hands until you feel a light stretch in the wrists. Hold for 10 seconds, repeat 5 times.</li>
          <li><strong>Wrist stretch.</strong> Stretch one arm forward, palm down. With the other hand gently bend the hand down until you feel a stretch. Hold for 15 seconds, change hands, repeat 3 times.</li>
          <li><strong>Finger spread.</strong> Make a fist, then open it quickly and spread the fingers as wide as you can. 10 times.</li>
          <li><strong>Wrist circles.</strong> Stretch your arms forward and close your fingers. Rotate the hands clockwise and anticlockwise, 10 times each way.</li>
          <li><strong>“Spider on glass”.</strong> Rest your fingertips on a flat surface and slowly “crawl” down with the fingers without lifting them. 10 times.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Do the exercises every day for 10–14 days. After surgery or an injection, start when the wound has healed. If numbness and pain do not go away or get worse, see a doctor.</p>
        <p><a href={`${tg}/28`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">A video of these exercises</a> is on my Telegram channel (in Russian).</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <p>It takes time — usually several weeks — to see whether treatment works. If things improve, we continue. If not, we discuss surgery.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>When surgery is needed</h2>
    <p>The key question is how long the nerve has been compressed and what is happening to it. I recommend surgery if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>symptoms have lasted several months and conservative treatment does not help;</li>
      <li>numbness has become constant or is getting worse;</li>
      <li>hand weakness has appeared or the muscles at the base of the thumb have wasted;</li>
      <li>the nerve conduction study shows marked compression.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In such cases it is not worth waiting. With long compression, changes occur in the nerve that cannot be reversed, and even after a well-performed operation strength and sensation do not fully recover.</p>
    <h3>How the operation is done</h3>
    <p>The operation is called <strong>median nerve decompression</strong> (carpal tunnel release). The idea is simple: divide the ligament pressing on the nerve and give it room.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Local anaesthesia — an injection in the wrist area.</li>
      <li>An incision of about 2–3 cm in the palm. I place it along a natural skin crease, so after healing the scar is usually hard to see.</li>
      <li>The transverse carpal ligament is divided along its whole length, and the nerve is freed.</li>
      <li>The wound is closed with a cosmetic suture and dressed.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The whole operation takes 15–20 minutes, and you can move your fingers straight away. In a public hospital it is done with a hospital stay. In some cases you can go home right after the operation — we will discuss this at the appointment.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Endoscopic surgery</h3>
      <p>This is a less traumatic option: I divide the ligament through a smaller incision, using a camera. <strong>Endoscopic release</strong> of the median nerve is a paid operation and costs more. We will discuss at the appointment whether it suits you.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="video-card" href={`${tg}/16`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <span class="vthumb"><img src="/media/surgery.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></span>
      <span class="vtext"><b>Video of carpal tunnel surgery</b><small>Watch on my Telegram channel (in Russian). Contains surgical footage.</small></span>
